    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse",
    "problem": "Determine the length of the minor axis of the ellipse whose equation is  $25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100=0.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100 & =0 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}+4x]+4[{y}^{2}-10y]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+4[(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-4]+4[(y-5)^{2}-25]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =-100+100+100 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5>2,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4.$",
